<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the hardened runtime, which notarization requires.

  This file starts EMPTY on purpose. zed's own entitlements
  (crates/zed/resources/zed.entitlements in the pinned checkout) are not a
  template: most of its entries — camera, audio input, address book, calendars,
  location, photos — exist for collab and screen-sharing features that this app
  deliberately keeps inert via BlockedHttpClient (workspace_host.rs:72), and its
  files.user-selected.read-write entries are App Sandbox keys that do nothing in
  an unsandboxed app. Copying them would request privacy-sensitive entitlements
  we never use and leave nobody able to explain any line.

  Add an entitlement ONLY when a hardened-runtime build demonstrably fails
  without it, and record the observed failure in a comment beside it. The
  likeliest candidates, if they turn out to be needed at all, are:

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory

  both for the tree-sitter (0.26) -> wasmtime (36) path. That JIT may never
  execute, since zed bundles native grammars — which is exactly why this is
  settled by measurement rather than by assumption.

  App Sandbox is NOT adopted: notarization does not require it, and it is
  incompatible with spawning agents, shells, and git across arbitrary worktrees.

  Design: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-04-signing-notarization-design.md
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
